+
+# Large Carrier Common Core, Open Interfaces, and Role Envelope Runtime
+
+## Runtime Purpose
+
+Define a shared technical grammar that lets large carriers exchange parts, host smaller bases and satisfy common safety obligations without collapsing into one silhouette or one optimal build.
+
+The runtime separates:
+
+- immutable physical requirements;
+- standardized interoperability surfaces;
+- hull-lineage decisions;
+- mission-profile allocations;
+- installed modules;
+- temporary cargo and carried craft;
+- cultural and historical expression.
+
+# 1. Carrier Entity Model
+
+A carrier is represented as a persistent graph of districts, modules, trunks, boundaries and interfaces.
+
+```text
+CarrierProfile
+├── identity and worldline provenance
+├── hull lineage and construction generation
+├── structural graph
+├── pressure and atmosphere graph
+├── power and thermal graph
+├── water, food and waste graph
+├── transit and docking graph
+├── information and command graph
+├── districts and population
+├── mission emphasis profile
+├── installed modules
+├── carried-base manifest
+├── legal and governance state
+├── maintenance and damage state
+└── historical modifications
+```
+
+No single `ship_type` field determines capability.
+
+# 2. Common Civic Core
+
+Every inhabited large carrier must declare capacity in twelve domains.
+
+| Domain | Minimum runtime outputs |
+|---|---|
+| Habitation | safe berths, privacy classes, accessibility, common space |
+| Life support | atmosphere, water, food reserve, waste closure |
+| Mobility | propulsion, attitude control, navigation, traffic safety |
+| Energy and heat | generation, storage, distribution, radiator margin |
+| Repair and fabrication | inspection, spare production, structural intervention |
+| Care | clinic, emergency surgery or stabilization, quarantine |
+| Logistics | cargo, stores, inventory provenance, material handling |
+| Information | sensing, communications, records, local computation |
+| Governance | authority graph, audit, deliberation and emergency procedures |
+| Culture and education | learning, recreation, ritual and artistic production |
+| Rescue and emergency | shelter, fire control, escape, casualty movement |
+| Defense | warning, hardening, cyber/physical containment, proportionate force |
+
+Each domain reports:
+
+- installed capacity;
+- effective capacity;
+- reserve;
+- dependencies;
+- qualified staff;
+- automation coverage;
+- current demand;
+- legal minimum;
+- failure consequences.
+
+# 3. Role Envelope
+
+A mission profile is a vector of emphasis weights, not a class identifier.
+
+Example domains:
+
+```text
+habitation
+survey
+care
+fabrication
+trade
+route_engineering
+security
+planetary_interface
+ecological_continuity
+education_and_culture
+```
+
+Weights influence design targets and scheduling priority, but they cannot authorize deletion of common-core functions.
+
+## 3.1 Allocation Constraints
+
+The allocator must enforce:
+
+- total configurable capacity is bounded;
+- common-core minimums are reserved before emphasis allocation;
+- reserve margins are explicit;
+- staffing and maintenance scale with installed systems;
+- added capability consumes mass, volume, heat, power and attention;
+- redundancy is not free;
+- carried craft consume dock, handling and support capacity;
+- emergency surge capacity cannot be treated as normal throughput indefinitely.
+
+## 3.2 Profile Blending
+
+A carrier may blend several emphases.
+
+Examples:
+
+- 0.30 habitation, 0.20 survey, 0.20 fabrication, 0.15 care, 0.15 trade;
+- 0.25 route engineering, 0.20 fabrication, 0.20 security, 0.20 logistics, 0.15 habitation;
+- 0.25 ecological continuity, 0.20 habitation, 0.20 education, 0.20 planetary interface, 0.15 care.
+
+Profiles may change through refit, emergency conversion, mission transition or political decision.
+
+# 4. Standard Interface Families
+
+## 4.1 Structural Interfaces
+
+Standardize:
+
+- attachment load envelopes;
+- capture and docking geometry classes;
+- pressure-boundary mating rules;
+- cargo restraint and handling points;
+- emergency tow points;
+- inspection and nondestructive-testing markers.
+
+Do not standardize the whole hull.
+
+## 4.2 Utility Interfaces
+
+Every compatible interface declares:
+
+- voltage, frequency and conversion limits;
+- thermal loop temperature and pressure bands;
+- atmosphere composition and contamination state;
+- potable, process and waste-water classes;
+- data bandwidth and trust domain;
+- time synchronization and causal epoch;
+- emergency shutdown authority;
+- metering and ownership.
+
+## 4.3 Human and Multispecies Interfaces
+
+Compatibility includes:
+
+- physical access geometry;
+- pressure and atmosphere;
+- gravity and acceleration tolerance;
+- sensory safety;
+- pathogen and biochemical boundaries;
+- machine-body service needs;
+- privacy and legal jurisdiction.
+
+A docking collar may be mechanically compatible while socially or biologically unsafe.
+
+# 5. Hull Lineage Parameters
+
+A hull lineage defines constraints and tendencies rather than a fixed model.
+
+Parameters include:
+
+- load path topology;
+- pressure-volume arrangement;
+- distributed versus centralized propulsion;
+- gravity strategy;
+- radiator architecture;
+- armor and shielding philosophy;
+- internal district topology;
+- docking orientation;
+- construction material families;
+- field repair access;
+- expansion surfaces;
+- design life;
+- fabrication method;
+- home yard and standards tradition.
+
+A lineage may generate many visually and functionally distinct ships.
+
+# 6. Internal City Topologies
+
+Supported patterns should include:
+
+## Spine
+
+A central transit and utility axis with districts attached along its length.
+
+Strengths: legibility, expansion, cargo movement.
+Weaknesses: chokepoints and long transit distances.
+
+## Ring
+
+Circular or toroidal transit with distributed districts.
+
+Strengths: redundancy and possible rotational gravity.
+Weaknesses: complex construction and docking.
+
+## Cluster
+
+Several semi-independent habitat and work nodes.
+
+Strengths: isolation and graceful degradation.
+Weaknesses: duplicated services and coordination overhead.
+
+## Courtyard
+
+Large shared pressure volumes surrounded by workshops and homes.
+
+Strengths: civic life and visual identity.
+Weaknesses: pressure-volume risk and high structural demand.
+
+## Raft
+
+Parallel modules connected by trusses and utility bridges.
+
+Strengths: modular growth and exterior work access.
+Weaknesses: radiation, micrometeoroid exposure and transit complexity.
+
+## Symbiotic or grown topology
+
+Living tissues, machine swarms or adaptive structures change arrangement over time.
+
+Strengths and weaknesses depend on organism agency, growth limits and maintenance ecology.
+
+# 7. Capacity Accounting
+
+The runtime must track at least:
+
+- dry mass;
+- propellant and reaction mass;
+- consumables;
+- cargo mass and volume;
+- rotating mass;
+- center of mass and inertia tensor;
+- thrust and acceleration envelope;
+- power generation and storage;
+- peak and sustained thermal rejection;
+- pressure volume;
+- shielding distribution;
+- crew time;
+- maintenance debt;
+- dock cycle capacity;
+- fabrication throughput;
+- medical and shelter surge capacity;
+- food and water closure;
+- spare-part diversity.
+
+## Crew Attention as a Resource
+
+Installed systems require:
+
+- operators;
+- maintainers;
+- inspectors;
+- planners;
+- governance;
+- emergency response.
+
+Automation reduces routine attention but creates verification, cybersecurity, repair and authority requirements.
+
+# 8. Configuration and Refit
+
+Refit classes:
+
+### Soft configuration
+
+Software, stores, staffing, carried craft and deployable equipment change without structural work.
+
+### Module exchange
+
+Dock or yard replaces compatible modules.
+
+### District conversion
+
+A pressure volume changes function, requiring construction, relocation and social negotiation.
+
+### Structural refit
+
+Load paths, propulsion, radiators or major docking systems change in a shipyard.
+
+### Lineage transformation
+
+The carrier is rebuilt across generations while preserving identity and provenance.
+
+Each refit records:
+
+- removed capability;
+- new dependencies;
+- displaced residents or institutions;
+- ownership of installed work;
+- certification state;
+- historical continuity claims.
+
+# 9. Variant Generation
+
+Procedural variation should operate over bounded authored grammars.
+
+A generated carrier can vary:
+
+- number and arrangement of districts;
+- module manufacturers and eras;
+- radiator and propulsion placement;
+- external vehicle racks;
+- repair patches;
+- paint, symbols and surface culture;
+- window and public-space distribution;
+- garden strategy;
+- docking mix;
+- legacy components;
+- acoustic signature;
+- lighting and wayfinding;
+- mission-profile allocation;
+- legal charter;
+- crew composition.
+
+Generation must pass engineering and civic constraints before acceptance.
+
+# 10. Anti-Metagame Rules
+
+The runtime must resist one universal best carrier.
+
+- Mission value depends on route, environment, crew and politics.
+- Large unused capacity still costs mass and maintenance.
+- Extreme modularity reduces structural and volumetric efficiency.
+- Highly integrated hulls gain efficiency but become harder to refit.
+- Common parts ease logistics but may be locally unavailable.
+- Rare technology creates dependency and political attention.
+- Weapon capacity creates legal and diplomatic cost.
+- Luxury increases morale and legitimacy for some communities but consumes scarce capacity.
+- Redundancy improves survival but can reduce peak mission throughput.
+
+# 11. Determinism and Multiplayer
+
+Authoritative state includes:
+
+- profile identifier;
+- module graph hashes;
+- mission weights;
+- installed and effective capacities;
+- docked-craft state;
+- ownership and authority references;
+- damage and maintenance debt;
+- configuration epoch.
+
+Clients may stream presentation detail, but cannot independently decide capacity or docking state.
+
+# 12. Debug Explainability
+
+A developer view must answer:
+
+- Why can this carrier perform this role?
+- Which modules and staff enable it?
+- Which common-core minimum would be violated by this refit?
+- What consumes the current heat, power and crew budget?
+- Why can this craft dock but not safely exchange atmosphere?
+- Which mission profile influenced this generated layout?
+- What capability would be lost if a district were isolated?
+
+## Runtime Maxim
+
+> **Standardize the handshakes, not the civilizations.**
